    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "What it works out",
      "items": [
        "LINEAR: y = y[i] + (y[i+1] - y[i]) * t, with t the fraction between",
        "PCHIP: a cubic whose slopes keep it inside the range of the two points",
        "SPLINE: a cubic whose curvature matches at every point"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "The difference that matters",
      "items": [
        "A spline is smoother, and to be that smooth it MAY OVERSHOOT",
        "Given points that rise then flatten, it can rise above the highest of them",
        "PCHIP gives up some smoothness and never leaves the range of the data"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Which to take",
      "items": [
        "A physical limit, a calibration or a duty cycle should be read with PCHIP",
        "A curve that must look right to the eye should be read with a spline",
        "Where the table is dense, linear's corner is smaller than the noise"
